WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:13.440
In September of 2017, Puerto Rico suffered
its most significant natural disaster in the

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.170
last 100 years.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:23.420
The impact of hurricane Maria, a huge category
4 cyclone--a category 5 at its strongest--

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:28.920
with wind gusts of over 155 mph and torrential
rains, wreaked havoc on the island.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.530
The destruction was enormous.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:35.640
Thousands of houses were destroyed and the
entire Puerto Rican electricity network collapsed.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.890
The island was, quite literally, engulfed
in darkness.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:41.140
As you can imagine, the economic damage was
enormous.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:47.430
To give you an idea, today the reconstruction
costs have risen to around 100 billion dollars.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:52.570
And that’s not all.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:57.280
Seven months later, thousands of businesses
remain closed and blackouts are still quite

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.280
frequent.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.970
However, dear viewer, wait just a moment,
because Hurricane Maria isn’t the whole

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:05.309
story.

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:12.910
The truth is that the island’s problems
began a long time ago.

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:17.320
Puerto Rico has been suffering a severe economic
crisis and a population exodus for more than

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.390
10 years, an exodus so great that it threatens
the island’s very survival.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:28.650
See, after declaring bankruptcy, in May of
2017, the situation is so critical that the

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:33.060
island's government is considering moving
thousands of prisoners to private prisons

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.600
in the United States.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:35.600
Why?

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.770
The island's government is even having problems
with financing its penitentiary system, and

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:45.250
“renting” spots in places such as Texas
or Florida is cheaper.

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:50.410
Just a moment.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:51.430
Prisoners to the United States?

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:53.510
Yes, you heard that right.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:58.560
Don’t forget that Puerto Rico is part of
the United States, not as a state, but as

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:06.400
a kind of "free associated state".

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:23.689
And of course, by being part of the United
States, having a tropical climate, a rich

00:02:23.689 --> 00:02:28.049
history and beautiful white sand beaches on
which, by the way, Captain Jack Sparrow had

00:02:28.049 --> 00:02:31.879
some of his most famous exploits, we might
think that Puerto Rico has everything required

00:02:31.879 --> 00:02:35.749
to be one of the most prosperous places in
the world, right?

00:02:35.749 --> 00:02:42.650
Well… not so much; the truth is actually
quite different.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:51.180
My friends, here’s a question… where do
you think most Puerto Ricans live?

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.540
Sounds like a silly question, right?

00:02:53.540 --> 00:03:03.060
Well, it isn’t... because the answer isn’t
Puerto Rico.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:04.060
THE ESCAPE

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:11.379
Yes, at first Puerto Rico may look like a
paradise: good weather, beautiful beaches,

00:03:11.379 --> 00:03:12.609
wonderful music...

00:03:12.609 --> 00:03:13.609
However...

00:03:13.609 --> 00:03:18.359
the island has a huge problem: every year,
thousands and thousands of Puerto Ricans leave

00:03:18.359 --> 00:03:19.359
the island.

00:03:19.359 --> 00:03:23.859
See, Puerto Ricans have been US citizens since
1917, they are Americans...

00:03:23.859 --> 00:03:26.549
so they can move around the country freely.

00:03:26.549 --> 00:03:31.450
Well, according to data from the Pew Research
Center, during the last decade more than 500,000

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:36.329
Puerto Ricans have left Puerto Rico to move
to the continent, to places like Texas, Florida,

00:03:36.329 --> 00:03:38.060
New York and Pennsylvania.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:39.989
500,000 people!

00:03:39.989 --> 00:03:45.540
That is a huge number for an island that has
just over 3 million inhabitants.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:53.459
Seen in another way, only in the last 10 years
1 of every 7 inhabitants has left Puerto Rico.

00:03:53.459 --> 00:03:55.390
Remember the question I just asked?

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:57.029
Where did you think the most Puerto Ricans
lived?

00:03:57.029 --> 00:04:01.650
Well, the answer is that since 2006, there
are more Puerto Ricans living in the continental

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.049
United States than on the island.

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:07.849
Specifically more than 5 million compared
to just over 3.

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:13.829
"We are watching a real live demographic and
population collapse on a monumental scale".

00:04:13.829 --> 00:04:19.340
Lyman Stone, an independent migration researcher
and economist at the Agriculture Department.)

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:22.310
And things could get much, much worse.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:26.610
The island's own government expects, just
in 2018, for another 200,000 - 200,000!!

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.660
- Puerto Ricans to leave.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:35.700
This means that Puerto Rico will lose about
6% of its population, in a single year!

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:43.970
My friends… if you thought this process,
this exodus, was already vertiginous, the

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.800
consequences of Hurricane Maria have only
accelerated it.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.340
Different estimates, both from the government
and from private consultants, expect another

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:55.500
half million Puerto Ricans to pack their bags
and move to the continent in the next 5 years.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:00.030
“I am very attached to my island, and my
preference is to stay here, but I have to

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.100
think what’s best for my son”.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:06.540
Victor Dominguez, a 39-year-old mortgage banker
to Washington Post)

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:10.650
As you may imagine, such a flight will have
very serious consequences for the island,

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.940
especially because most of those who are leaving
are young.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:16.860
In fact, it is estimated that half of all
emigrants, of all people who leave the island,

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:18.980
are under 24 years of age.

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:20.420
The result?

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:26.040
Well... in the next decade, Puerto Rico’s
population will decrease to 2.5 million people.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:31.450
That is to say, in the next decade Puerto
Rico will lose 800,000 inhabitants, 25% of

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:33.010
its population.

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:37.700
Just listen to Lyman Stone’s forecast.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:42.101
And, if another natural disaster, another
hurricane for example, occurs or if the island

00:05:42.101 --> 00:05:46.260
fails to recover from the severe crisis it’s
going through... the reality could be even

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:47.260
worse...

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.480
Can you imagine what this will mean for the
island’s economy?

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:51.420
Who’s going to pay its huge debt?

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:55.990
Who will pay for the pensions?

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:57.460
But...

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:00.400
my friends...

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.240
at this point...

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.860
I think we need to ask a question, why do
so many Puerto Ricans leave?

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:10.050
The statistics are typical of a country at
war... but... there’s no war in Puerto Rico.

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:11.600
So what's going on?

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:15.760
Listen up.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:21.540
(A VERY LONG CRISIS)

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.570
Why are so many people leaving Puerto Rico?

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:27.850
Folks… if we take a look at the data...
the explanation seems very clear.

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:31.530
Almost half of the Puerto Ricans living on
the island are considered poor – under US

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:32.530
standards, of course.

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:36.590
The average family income is just $ 20,000,
which is slightly less than half the average

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:41.940
income of Mississippi, the poorest state in
the continental United States.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:53.490
But that’s not all, the island’s government
is bankrupt and has a huge public debt: 70

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:57.840
billion dollars and another 50 billion in
payment commitments like, for example, pensions.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:04.700
Remember that we’re talking about an island
of just over 3 million inhabitants!

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:09.970
That’s crazy.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:14.470
This means that an average family would have
to devote 6 years of their salary to pay the

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:15.470
public debt...

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:20.040
Now you can understand why Puerto Rico is
known as the Greece of the Caribbean.

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:24.530
And if you think this is the worst of it,
Well... you're wrong.

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:28.051
The worst part is that there’s hardly any
work in Puerto Rico... in fact, the island

00:07:28.051 --> 00:07:30.920
has the lowest activity rate in the United
States.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:33.940
Only 4 out of 10 adults work or are looking
for work.

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:37.340
And if we don’t count the people who don’t
have a job and public workers – whose salaries

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:41.790
come out of the taxes of the people who work
in the private sector – the result is alarming;

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:48.030
less than a quarter of the island’s inhabitants
work in the private sector.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:53.420
By the way, one of Puerto Rico’s great problems
is precisely this: public employees, officials.

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:58.250
See, almost 1 out of every 4 jobs on the island
corresponds to the government and that’s

00:07:58.250 --> 00:07:59.620
not the worst part...

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:05.670
Did you know that many of these public employees
had the right to retire at 50, 15 years earlier

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:13.830
than in the rest of the United States?

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:18.060
And if you like scary movies, you can also
keep in mind that given the high percentage

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:21.840
of young people leaving… the island’s
population is ageing very, very quickly.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.000
In other words...

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.430
if things don’t change...

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:27.790
Puerto Rico will enter a very, very difficult
situation...

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:31.810
But okay, at this point, I'm sure you're wondering,
how on earth can the island get out of this

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:32.810
mess?

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:35.750
Is there really a way out?

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:39.700
Let's see.

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:43.720
(HOW TO GET OUT OF THIS?)

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:49.500
It’s often said that those who don’t learn
from history are condemned to repeat it.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:50.960
This may happen in Puerto Rico.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:53.920
When we talk about how local politicians see
the future...

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:56.900
we find some good things, and some not so
good things.

00:08:56.900 --> 00:09:01.020
Puerto Rico has gone bankrupt, that is a fact...
and if they continue like this the government

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:06.010
could push the island into the abyss, so,
for one, public spending has to be cut, to

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:11.760
stop the drainage that has caused such a huge
amount of public debt.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.650
And in principle, the new governor, Ricardo
Rosselló, is working on this, in fact he

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:19.561
has committed to decreasing the government’s
size, by a lot… the problem is that how

00:09:19.561 --> 00:09:24.080
he wants to do it isn’t quite clear, that
is, it’s not clear if there’s a real political

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.290
will to correct the island’s massive problems.

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:33.470
For another, Puerto Rico has to turn its economic
policy around and set some serious plans into

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:36.040
motion to attract investments.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:41.270
Because Puerto Rico needs decisive measures,
real reforms, not cosmetic changes.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:45.130
A few things have been done in recent years…
for example, they are trying to turn Puerto

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:56.060
Rico into a kind of financial centre that
guarantees anonymity to international investors.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:58.800
This could work, but there’s a long way
to go...

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.370
Now, before we say goodbye, we still need
to ask a very important question.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:06.510
How on earth did Puerto Rico get to this point?

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:11.260
Well, one reason was that local governments
spent huge amounts of money to hire, as we

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:14.381
saw, many public workers, and to fund expensive
projects, pharaonic tasks that didn’t give

00:10:14.381 --> 00:10:18.740
the island anything other than more debt.

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:23.500
The other reason has a lot to do with the
US’s influence, but we’ll tell you all

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:24.900
about that in an upcoming video.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:25.900
Now it’s your turn.

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:27.920
And this time I'm really going to ask you
to jump into it:

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:32.870
Do you think that Puerto Rico should continue
to belong to the United States or should they

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:35.170
seek to become an independent country?

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:37.540
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:40.950
I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did and don’t forget to

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:44.050
subscribe to our channel for brand new videos
